{
  "requirement": "`Comment` covers source comments, including documentation comments.\n`Annotation` covers language constructs such as attributes and decorators\nthat annotate a declaration. Providers use the common kind when it applies\nand `Other` with a language-defined name for additional kinds.\n\n```rust,ignore\n#[non_exhaustive]\npub enum AttachmentKind {\n    Comment,\n    Annotation,\n    Other(String),\n}\n```\n\nTrait implementations\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl Clone for AttachmentKind;\nimpl Debug for AttachmentKind;\nimpl PartialEq for AttachmentKind;\nimpl Eq for AttachmentKind;\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The tests adequately check `requirements.sdk.outlines.AttachmentKind`.\n\n**Behavioral obligations**\n- **`Comment` covers source comments, including documentation comments.**\n  - `tests/outline_content.rs#fn[rust_attachments_keep_the_owner_kind_and_original_bytes]` asserts `Comment` for `//!` and `///` doc comments and for a trailing `//` comment.\n  - `fn[bundled_languages_classify_only_their_attachment_syntax]` asserts `Comment` for Rust block comments (`/*!`, `/**`, `/*`), Python `#`, TS/TSX, CSS, YAML, TOML and Markdown HTML comments. It also asserts no attachments for plain text.\n- **`Annotation` covers attributes and decorators.**\n  - The Rust test asserts `Annotation` for `#[inline]`.\n  - The bundled-languages test asserts `Annotation` for the Python decorators `@a`, `@b(1)` and the multi-line `@pkg.decorate(...)`.\n- **Providers use `Other` with a language-defined name for additional kinds.**\n  - `fn[custom_attachment_spans_are_checked_against_the_original_snapshot]` uses a custom `OutlineLang` that emits `AttachmentKind::Other(\"marker\".into())`. It asserts that the kind round-trips unchanged through `OutlineContent`.\n  - `fn[outline_constructors_preserve_fields_and_replace_attachments]` also asserts the `Annotation` kind.\n  - Neither test carries a `{test}` reference to the subject, but both are only corroboration. The marked tests already cover the obligation.\n- **Providers use the common kind when it applies.** The classification assertions above check this. A provider that emitted `Other` for a comment or decorator would fail them.\n\n**Type-shape obligations**\n- **Enum shape, `#[non_exhaustive]` and trait impls.**\n  - `tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]` calls `check_declaration`. That function compares rustdoc's published trait impls with the requirement's \"Trait implementations\" listing, which names `Clone`, `Debug`, `PartialEq` and `Eq`.\n  - It uses exact set equality, so a missing `Eq` or an extra impl fails the test.\n  - It also compares rustdoc's item declaration with the requirement's declaration through `signature_parts`. The declaration includes the `#[non_exhaustive]` attribute and the variants `Comment`, `Annotation` and `Other(String)`.\n- **Uses of the traits in other tests.** The behavioral tests also use `Clone` (`a.kind.clone()`), and `PartialEq` and `Debug` (`assert_eq!` on kinds).\n\nI found no uncovered obligation or boundary, and there is no `{fixme}` to account for.",
  "references": "a0eb425b7335bdba3162ac71ca4bac1d2b4dea507b6f6bb4c82bdc79e416b394",
